window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.8","title":"Effect Size and Practical Significance","goal":"Compute Cohen's d for the memory-training study by hand, then see a huge sample make a tiny effect significant while d stays tiny.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the two groups","text":"The memory-training study from the lesson: 25 people trained, 25 controls. The scores below are built to match the lesson (trained: mean 78, SD 10; control: mean 70, SD 12).","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ntrained = np.array([95.69, 56.78, 81.95, 73.61, 74.58, 76.59, 61.32, 76.45, 71.09, 106.54, 80.32, 75.43, 76.03, 72.76, 69.48, 75.11, 82.49, 76.39, 86.52, 76.72, 78.62, 91.5, 83.03, 74.14, 76.87])\ncontrol = np.array([62.92, 68.16, 88.37, 77.33, 52.05, 70.03, 63.23, 71.72, 52.41, 72.74, 72.67, 87.4, 73.57, 75.7, 53.68, 94.84, 49.03, 82.19, 66.46, 60.41, 62.87, 62.7, 74.26, 68.88, 86.38])\n\nfor name, g in [('trained', trained), ('control', control)]:\n    print(f'{name}: n = {len(g)}, mean = {g.mean():.2f}, sd = {g.std(ddof=1):.2f}')","out":"trained: n = 25, mean = 78.00, sd = 10.00\ncontrol: n = 25, mean = 70.00, sd = 12.00","figs":[]},{"title":"Cohen's d by hand","text":"Cohen's d is the gap between the means measured in pooled standard deviations. The pooled SD is a weighted average of the two group SDs, so d does not depend on the sample size.","code":"n1, n2 = len(trained), len(control)\ns1, s2 = trained.std(ddof=1), control.std(ddof=1)\n\nsp = np.sqrt(((n1 - 1) * s1**2 + (n2 - 1) * s2**2) / (n1 + n2 - 2))\nd = (trained.mean() - control.mean()) / sp\ng = d * (1 - 3 / (4 * (n1 + n2 - 2) - 1))   # Hedges' g, a small-sample correction\n\nprint(f'pooled SD = {sp:.3f}')\nprint(f\"Cohen's d = {d:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Hedges' g = {g:.3f}\")","out":"pooled SD = 11.045\nCohen's d = 0.724\nHedges' g = 0.713","figs":[]},{"title":"Add the p-value","text":"The t-test says whether the gap could be chance, while d says how big it is. Report both. (This is the pooled test; the lesson's Welch version gives the same p to three decimals.)","code":"res = stats.ttest_ind(trained, control)\n\nprint(f't = {res.statistic:.3f}, df = {res.df:.0f}, p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"t = 2.561, df = 48, p = 0.0136","figs":[]},{"title":"Tiny effect, huge sample","text":"ttest_ind_from_stats runs the t-test from summary numbers. Compare three studies: a huge sample with a trivial gap, the memory study, and a tiny sample with a big gap. The p-values and d tell different stories.","code":"def study(label, diff, sd, n):\n    res = stats.ttest_ind_from_stats(diff, sd, n, 0, sd, n)   # pooled test\n    print(f'{label:<14} d = {diff / sd:.3f}  t = {res.statistic:.2f}  p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\n\nstudy('huge sample', 0.2, 15, 50000)\nstudy('memory study', 8, sp, 25)\nstudy('tiny sample', 15, 15, 4)","out":"huge sample    d = 0.013  t = 2.11  p = 0.0350\nmemory study   d = 0.724  t = 2.56  p = 0.0136\ntiny sample    d = 1.000  t = 1.41  p = 0.2070","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"trained <- c(95.69, 56.78, 81.95, 73.61, 74.58, 76.59, 61.32, 76.45, 71.09, 106.54, 80.32, 75.43, 76.03, 72.76, 69.48, 75.11, 82.49, 76.39, 86.52, 76.72, 78.62, 91.5, 83.03, 74.14, 76.87)\ncontrol <- c(62.92, 68.16, 88.37, 77.33, 52.05, 70.03, 63.23, 71.72, 52.41, 72.74, 72.67, 87.4, 73.57, 75.7, 53.68, 94.84, 49.03, 82.19, 66.46, 60.41, 62.87, 62.7, 74.26, 68.88, 86.38)\nsp <- sqrt((24 * var(trained) + 24 * var(control)) / 48)\n(mean(trained) - mean(control)) / sp          # Cohen's d\nt.test(trained, control, var.equal = TRUE)\n\nstudy <- function(diff, sd, n) {\n  t <- diff / sqrt(2 * sd^2 / n)\n  c(d = diff / sd, t = t, p = 2 * pt(-abs(t), 2 * n - 2))\n}\nstudy(0.2, 15, 50000)\nstudy(15, 15, 4)","expect":"d = 0.7243; t = 2.5609, df = 48, p-value = 0.01364; huge sample: d = 0.0133, t = 2.108, p = 0.03502; tiny sample: d = 1, t = 1.414, p = 0.2070","note":"Base R has no Cohen's d command, so it is computed from the pooled SD. The helper function reproduces the huge-sample and tiny-sample studies (the memory study's t and p appear in the t.test output)."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > Independent-Samples T Test (version 27 and later also prints an effect-size table with Cohen's d)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / label n diff sd.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 50000 0.2 15\n2 25 8 11.0454\n3 4 15 15\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE d = diff / sd.\nCOMPUTE t = diff / SQRT(2 * sd**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE p = 2 * CDF.T(-ABS(t), 2 * n - 2).\nLIST label d t p.","note":"With raw data in two groups, the menu route above gives the t-test (and d in newer versions). The syntax shows the by-hand version for the three studies: 1 = huge sample, 2 = memory study, 3 = tiny sample. The pooled SD of the memory study (11.0454) comes from the lesson."},"stata":{"code":"esizei 25 78 10 25 70 12, cohensd      // Cohen's d from summary numbers\nttesti 25 78 10 25 70 12\nesizei 50000 0.2 15 50000 0 15, cohensd\nttesti 50000 0.2 15 50000 0 15","note":"esizei takes each group's size, mean and SD and prints Cohen's d; ttesti runs the pooled t-test. With raw data use esize twosample score, by(group) cohensd."},"try":["Change the huge sample size from 50,000 to 500 and predict what happens to p and to d.","In the tiny-sample study, raise n from 4 to 40 and predict which of d and p changes, then run it."]});
